{
  "requirement": "An mdBook chapter MAY declare a named definition using a fenced `{definition}` directive. Definitions MUST use the same chapter-qualified names, identifier rules, fence rules, and nonempty-body rules as requirements and examples. Names MUST be unique across all three kinds.\n\nEach definition MUST declare exactly one nonempty `:phrase:` option before its explanatory body. The phrase MUST be interpreted as plain text. Definitions MUST NOT require their own evidence records or determinations.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The four marked tests in `tests/definitions.rs` cover each obligation in `requirements.definitions.declaration`, and their assertions enforce the expected results.\n\n- **Exactly one nonempty `:phrase:` before the body, and a nonempty body.** `invalid_declarations_and_reference_targets_are_reported` loops over four bad definitions: a body with no phrase, an empty phrase, two phrase lines, and a phrase with no body. Each must produce an error naming the definition `bad.bad`. Each input is otherwise valid, so any such error shows the specific defect was rejected. The `src/terms.rs#fn[declaration]` logic has exactly these branches.\n- **Fence rules.** The same test checks that an unclosed definition reports a missing closing colon fence. `definitions_share_name_and_fence_rules_and_need_no_evidence` checks that a four-colon opening fence holds a shorter three-colon line in its body, with no error for `long.fenced`.\n- **Chapter-qualified name and identifier rules.** `definitions_share_name_and_fence_rules_and_need_no_evidence` puts a definition in a chapter whose path gives an invalid namespace (`bad dir`). It asserts the invalid definition name error for `bad dir.terms.a`. The definition goes through the same `valid_name` check as requirements, in `src/evidence.rs#fn[collect_definitions]`. That check has no definition-specific branch, so I did not require a separate test of each identifier shape.\n- **Uniqueness across all three kinds.** `duplicates_involving_named_definitions_name_the_first_declaration` covers requirement then definition, and definition then example. It asserts the full duplicate message, which names the kind and the first declaration site. `invalid_declarations_and_reference_targets_are_reported` also asserts `duplicate definition bad.x` for a requirement and a definition with the same name. A definition/definition duplicate has no test of its own. The duplicate check is one unconditional map-entry branch that does not depend on kind, so I do not count that as a gap.\n- **Phrase interpreted as plain text.** `phrases_are_plain_links_and_markdown_literals_are_preserved` declares the phrase `*alpha* & <beta>`. It asserts the rendered link text is escaped (`&#42;alpha&#42; &amp; &lt;beta&gt;`), so Markdown and HTML in the phrase are not interpreted. It also asserts the `:phrase:` line is not rendered into the definition chapter.\n- **No evidence records or determinations required.** `definitions_share_name_and_fence_rules_and_need_no_evidence` asserts that the evidence-missing error for `rules.ready` appears. That shows the evidence check ran. It also asserts that no error mentions the valid definition `long.fenced`, or `glossary.`, so definitions need no evidence files.\n\nNo obligation in the declaration text lacks a test, and there is no {fixme} acknowledgment to consider.",
  "references": "0ebe06cb70292780b68ceb55eb528f3677d360862e79ee24dbe91dc3d6a10933",
